{"name":"Pewter Tray","hex":"#bdc5c0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pewter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An alloy of approximately 93–98% tin and 1–2% copper, and the balance of antimony.","An alloy of tin and lead."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark, dull grey colour, like that of the metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pewter"},{"term":"tray","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small, typically rectangular or round, flat, and rigid object upon which things are carried.","The items on a full tray."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["to place (items) on a tray","to slide down a snow-covered hill on a tray from a cafeteria."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tray"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pewter","blocks":[{"text":"Pewter is a malleable metal alloy consisting of tin (85–99%), antimony, copper (2%), bismuth, and sometimes silver. In the past, it was an alloy of tin and as much as 40% lead, but most modern pewter, in order to prevent lead poisoning, is not made with lead. Pewter has a low melting point, around 170–230 °C (338–446 °F), depending on the exact mixture of metals. The word pewter is possibly a variation of \"spelter\", a term for zinc alloys."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pewter","wikidata":"Q852372"},{"title":"Tray","blocks":[{"text":"A tray is a shallow platform designed for the carrying of items. It can be fashioned from numerous materials, including silver, brass, sheet iron, paperboard, wood, melamine, and molded pulp. Trays range in cost from inexpensive molded pulp trays which are disposable and inexpensive melamine trays used in cafeterias, to mid-priced wooden trays used in a home, to expensive silver trays used in luxury hotels. Some examples have raised galleries, handles, and short feet for support."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Brass_tray_inlaid_with_silver%2C_Egypt_or_Syria%2C_19th_century%2C_HAA_I.JPG/330px-Brass_tray_inlaid_with_silver%2C_Egypt_or_Syria%2C_19th_century%2C_HAA_I.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tray","wikidata":"Q613972"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Fork-Viking-Pewter-detail.jpg/250px-Fork-Viking-Pewter-detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Fork-Viking-Pewter-detail.jpg/250px-Fork-Viking-Pewter-detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Detail on a pewter fork handle from Norway, showing three scenes: King Olaf II Haraldsson (St. Olaf), his men, and a Viking ship","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFork-Viking-Pewter-detail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Pewter_Ring.jpg/250px-Pewter_Ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Pewter_Ring.jpg/330px-Pewter_Ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Pewter_Ring.jpg/250px-Pewter_Ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APewter_Ring.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Pewterplate_exb.jpg/250px-Pewterplate_exb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Pewterplate_exb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Pewterplate_exb.jpg/250px-Pewterplate_exb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"htt